story, began with early research in natural language processing and question-answering systems. Though Watson\u2019s commercial applications are well-known, its origins at MIT highlight the institute\u2019s long-standing commitment to pushing the boundaries of what AI systems can achieve. More recently, MIT researchers have contributed to the development of transformer models, the architecture behind modern large language models like BERT and GPT. CSAIL\u2019s work on efficient language models, such as the development of smaller, more interpretable models, has addressed critical challenges in scalability and computational efficiency, making AI more accessible and sustainable for real-world use.\n\nThe institute\u2019s commitment to AI education has ensured that its influence permeates the broader academic and industry landscape. MIT\u2019s OpenCourseWare initiative has made AI and machine learning courses freely available online, allowing global access to its educational resources. The MIT-IBM Watson AI Lab, established in 2017, has further strengthened the institute\u2019s role in AI by fostering collaboration between academia and industry, focusing on areas like AI hardware, healthcare, and climate science. This partnership has accelerated the translation of AI research into practical applications, demonstrating MIT\u2019s ability to bridge the gap between theoretical innovation and real-world impact. Additionally, MIT\u2019s AI-focused degree programs, such as the Master\u2019s in AI and the PhD in Electrical Engineering and Computer Science with a specialization in AI, have produced some of the most influential researchers and engineers in the field, ensuring that MIT\u2019s legacy in AI continues to grow.\n\nMIT\u2019s influence on AI is not limited to its direct research contributions; it has also shaped the ethical and societal discourse surrounding AI. The institute has been a leader in discussions about the responsible development and deployment of AI techn